[{"id":"VRCE-6-14-1","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":14,"verse":1,"english":"As Rāma slept on the earth on Kuśa spread over, without ever swerving from the rule, three nights passed away.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.864,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-21-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6014001","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5e70ff7557ee6b52c4bbbc0473e3b9e511832338a21545c3f09b3943d1c73a40","compact":"218140a853cd1dc9c6a34a17e183a56eab454a3cad51d687dae31b98634fd933","nasal":"218140a853cd1dc9c6a34a17e183a56eab454a3cad51d687dae31b98634fd933"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-14-2","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":14,"verse":2,"english":"me. And while spending three nights, Rāma, learned in polity and attached to virtue, prayed to that lord of streams-the Ocean. Yet the wicked Ocean, albeit adored according to his deserts by Rāma with his mind concentrated, did not show himself.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.538,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-21-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6014002","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0e5cc3d7243996b0a6f786cd2bd69ad8a71d7483c6294a634e269a11d3ad67cc","compact":"663fff62400f9602029f4cca8ecdf4195487160a5635f929995965d1b60b9a73","nasal":"f75f8e95f9bab702c5acec4a721745294c1d748f5c8baaa13711caeef69615c1"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-14-3","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":14,"verse":3,"english":"Thereat Răma grew enraged and had the corners of his eyes crimsoned; and he spank to Laksmana of auspicious marks, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.923,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-21-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6014003","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2d12d630fac1677c1574076c274ca3d5761bb64ac390172f9e9d5786412ec49d","compact":"b8bceeb78bac2a3ae3db93e948bd5c543edd285dbc4343de9f804f140c542ab4","nasal":"98b6512a16a09ab8951902f8ce1c852a0535ebe235dca6e60637a71c547a979c"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-14-4","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":14,"verse":4,"english":"Behold the hauteur of the Ocean in not presenting himself before Calmness, forbearance, candour, and soft speech-these virtues of the good are by the insolent taken for the effects of incompetency.","alignment":{"type":"approximate","confidence":0.186,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-21-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6014004","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"97ad0211c247b49870125f3d8bf664abb0ba8aecb1376dd1d9c766c9c8dce545","compact":"82f2a8f55fbd8cd2cf9f5ee5d1c18762a5323fe8ae85ce4c163292b75d4abd37","nasal":"82f2a8f55fbd8cd2cf9f5ee5d1c18762a5323fe8ae85ce4c163292b75d4abd37"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-14-5","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":14,"verse":5,"english":"Behold the hauteur of the Ocean in not presenting himself before Calmness, forbearance, candour, and soft speech-these virtues of the good are by the insolent taken for the effects of incompetency.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.8,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-21-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6014005","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"55fd2100f22cef961666b26f50b179bd35ca64a1829db00bef29481dd29981be","compact":"70b0eb032f5863699a77142e57a402ebb02759e5e2f869a2a5eb4692dd0bb4c9","nasal":"c39d9e377f348a30ea2fd1be1028fde8180ae5a57641413f896eb416a1e191db"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-14-6","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":14,"verse":6,"english":"The person' that is self-laudatory, wicked and badly-bold, publish his own praise and met out chastisement everywhere, is honoured in the world. 1. The word, however, is naram (ac.), man. 2. i.e. indiscriminately.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-21-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6014006","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"34d2dcbc37d4418957855fdeb90684c4fd3066b9ef06b8ef8174ad30a6659ab2","compact":"6a205ddd298603d65f78345b245d2029fc0f5b001403c8da3dd65958e80c4278","nasal":"d6617ce61c4275338f42e162686e851eb68aa9aa1089c7c1bfdc9b6866589e63"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-14-7","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":14,"verse":7,"english":"By moderation one cannot attain celebrity, by moderation one cannot attain fame, and, O Lakşmaņa, in this world by moderation one cannot attain victory on the edge of battle.* *Ranamārdhani, 'The perilous edge of battle when it raged.'","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.956,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-21-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6014007","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"94fc4c37ece3b17794f1cb57a8bfe81d4756ee2a192a7da756f6081e8206f086","compact":"4654769ab97e88bac5206100498bad9c04bd55d0596debac0030d002f6fc95fa","nasal":"60aaf81a5fd0499f965157a2df7bb801c9cce4989b946c8a29808df7a0539103"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-14-8","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":14,"verse":8,"english":"See, O Sumitrā's son, the abode of Makaras, with his waters obstructed all around with Makaras destroyed by my shafts. O Laksmana, see the bodies here of serpents, and the huge on bodies of mighty fishes, and the trunks of elephants, torn by my arrows.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.746,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-21-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6014008","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7f1b5fec9d6dcdbc53eaa4869575978374b758d1d820a5ac78dbd58b24b9c0c0","compact":"f82299c